(Page 15 of 54 - 01 - Introduction  to Agents) Tj T*
(Introduction to Agents) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(15) Tj T*
(Level 0: The Core Reasoning System) Tj T*
(Before we can have an agent, we must start with the "Brain" in its most basic form: the) Tj T*
(reasoning engine itself. In this configuration, a Language Model \(LM\) operates in isolation,) Tj T*
(responding solely based on its vast pre-trained knowledge without any tools, memory, or) Tj T*
(interaction with the live environment.) Tj T*
(Its strength lies in this extensive training, allowing it to explain established concepts and plan) Tj T*
(how to approach solving a problem with great depth. The trade-off is a complete lack of real-) Tj T*
(time awareness; it is functionally "blind" to any event or fact outside its training data.) Tj T*
(For instance, it can explain the rules of professional baseball and the complete history of the) Tj T*
(New York Yankees. But if you ask, "What was the final score of the Yankees game last night?",) Tj T*
(it would be unable to answer. That game is a specific, real-world event that happened after) Tj T*
(its training data was collected, so the information simply doesn't exist in its knowledge.) Tj T*
(Level 1: The Connected Problem-Solver) Tj T*
(At this level, the reasoning engine becomes a functional agent by connecting to and utilizing) Tj T*
(external tools - the "Hands" component of our architecture. Its problem-solving is no longer) Tj T*
(confined to its static, pre-trained knowledge.) Tj T*
(Using the 5-step loop, the agent can now answer our previous question. Given the "Mission":) Tj T*
("What was the final score of the Yankees game last night?", its "Think" step recognizes this) Tj T*
(as a real-time data need. Its "Act" step then invokes a tool, like a Google Search API with the) Tj T*
(proper date and search terms. It "Observes" the search result \(e.g., "Yankees won 5-3"\), and) Tj T*
(synthesizes that fact into a final answer.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
