(Page 19 of 54 - 01 - Introduction  to Agents) Tj T*
(Introduction to Agents) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(19) Tj T*
(Core Agent Architecture: Model, Tools,) Tj T*
(and Orchestration) Tj T*
(We know what an agent does and how it can scale. But how do we actually build it?) Tj T*
(The transition from concept to code lies in the specific architectural design of its three) Tj T*
(core components.) Tj T*
(Model: The "Brain" of your AI Agent) Tj T*
(The LM is the reasoning core of your agent, and its selection is a critical architectural) Tj T*
(decision that dictates your agent's cognitive capabilities, operational cost, and speed.) Tj T*
(However, treating this choice as a simple matter of picking the model with the highest) Tj T*
(benchmark score is a common path to failure. An agent's success in a production) Tj T*
(environment is rarely determined by generic academic benchmarks.) Tj T*
(Real-world success demands a model that excels at agentic fundamentals: superior) Tj T*
(reasoning to navigate complex, multi-step problems and reliable tool use to interact with) Tj T*
(the world7.) Tj T*
(To do this well, start by defining the business problem, then test models against metrics) Tj T*
(that directly map to that outcome. If your agent needs to write code, test it on your private) Tj T*
(codebase. If it processes insurance claims, evaluate its ability to extract information) Tj T*
(from your specific document formats. This analysis must then be cross-referenced with) Tj T*
(the practicalities of cost and latency. The "best" model is the one that sits at the optimal) Tj T*
(intersection of quality, speed, and price for your specific task8.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
