(Page 36 of 54 - 01 - Introduction  to Agents) Tj T*
(Introduction to Agents) Tj T*
(Updated May 2026) Tj T*
(36) Tj T*
(Agent Identity: A New Class of Principal) Tj T*
(In the traditional security model, there are human users which might use OAuth or SSO,) Tj T*
(and there are services which use IAM or service accounts. Agents add a 3rd category of) Tj T*
(principle. An agent is not merely a piece of code; it is an autonomous actor, a new kind of) Tj T*
(principal that requires its own verifiable identity. Just as employees are issued an ID badge,) Tj T*
(each agent on the platform must be issued a secure, verifiable "digital passport." This Agent) Tj T*
(Identity is distinct from the identity of the user who invoked it and the developer who built it.) Tj T*
(This is a fundamental shift in how we must approach Identity and Access Management \(IAM\)) Tj T*
(in the enterprise.) Tj T*
(Having each identity be verified and having access controls for all of them, is the bedrock) Tj T*
(of agent security. Once an agent has a cryptographically verifiable identity \(often using) Tj T*
(standards like SPIFFE35\), it can be granted its own specific, least-privilege permissions. The) Tj T*
(SalesAgent is granted read/write access to the CRM, while the HRonboardingAgent is) Tj T*
(explicitly denied. This granular control is critical. It ensures that even if a single agent is) Tj T*
(compromised or behaves unexpectedly, the potential blast radius is contained. Without an) Tj T*
(agent identity construct, agents cannot work on behalf of humans with limited delegated) Tj T*
(authority. The Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform now also natively enforces this via the Agent) Tj T*
(Identity feature, ensuring every agent receives a unique cryptographic ID. This creates a) Tj T*
(clear, auditable trail mapped back to defined enterprise authorization policies.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
