(Table 1: A non-exhaustive example of different categories of actors for authentication) Tj T*
(Policies to Constrain Access) Tj T*
(A policy is a form of authorization \(AuthZ\), distinct from authentication \(AuthN\). Typically,) Tj T*
(policies limit the capabilities of a principal; for example, "Users in Marketing can only access) Tj T*
(these 27 API endpoints and cannot execute DELETE commands." As we develop agents, we) Tj T*
(need to apply permissions to the agents, their tools, other internal agents, context they can) Tj T*
(share, and remote agents. Think about it this way: if you add all the APIs, data, tools, and) Tj T*
(agents to your system, then you must constrain access to a subset of just those capabilities) Tj T*
(required to get their jobs done. This is the recommended approach: applying the principle of) Tj T*
(least privilege while remaining contextually relevant.36) Tj T*
(Principal entity Authentication / Verification Notes) Tj T*
(Users Authenticated with OAuth) Tj T*
(or SSO) Tj T*
(Human actors with full) Tj T*
(autonomy and responsibility for) Tj T*
(their actions) Tj T*
(Agents \(new category of) Tj T*
(principles\)) Tj T*
(Verified with SPIFFE Agents have delegated) Tj T*
(authority, taking actions on) Tj T*
(behalf of users) Tj T*
(Service accounts Integrated into IAM Applications and containers,) Tj T*
(fully deterministic, no) Tj T*
(responsible for actions) Tj T*
ET
